      "metadata": {
        "children": [
          {
            "name": "title",
            "namespace": "http://oval.mitre.org/XMLSchema/oval-definitions-5",
            "text": "OL09-00-000286 - OL 9 must use the SSSD package for multifactor authentication services."
          },
          {
            "attributes": {
              "family": "unix"
            },
            "children": [
              {
                "name": "platform",
                "namespace": "http://oval.mitre.org/XMLSchema/oval-definitions-5",
                "text": "Oracle Linux 9"
              }
            ],
            "name": "affected",
            "namespace": "http://oval.mitre.org/XMLSchema/oval-definitions-5"
          },
          {
            "name": "description",
            "namespace": "http://oval.mitre.org/XMLSchema/oval-definitions-5",
            "text": "Using an authentication device, such as a CAC or token that is separate from the information system, ensures that even if the information system is compromised, that compromise will not affect credentials stored on the authentication device. \n\t \n\tMultifactor solutions that require devices separate from information systems gaining access include, for example, hardware tokens providing time-based or challenge-response authenticators and smart cards such as the U.S. Government Personal Identity Verification card and the DOD Common Access Card. \n\t \n\tA privileged account is defined as an information system account with authorizations of a privileged user. \n\t \n\tWithout the use of multifactor authentication, the ease of access to privileged functions is greatly increased. \n\t \n\tMultifactor authentication requires using two or more factors to achieve authentication. \n\t \n\tFactors include:  \n\t1) Something a user knows (e.g., password/PIN); \n\t2) Something a user has (e.g., cryptographic identification device, token); and \n\t3) Something a user is (e.g., biometric). \n\t \n\tA privileged account is defined as an information system account with authorizations of a privileged user. \n\t \n\tNetwork access is defined as access to an information system by a user (or a process acting on behalf of a user) communicating through a network (e.g., local area network, wide area network, or the internet). \n\t \n\tThe DOD common access card (CAC) with DOD-approved PKI is an example of multifactor authentication.\n\n\tSatisfies: SRG-OS-000705-GPOS-00150, SRG-OS-000105-GPOS-00052, SRG-OS-000106-GPOS-00053, SRG-OS-000107-GPOS-00054, SRG-OS-000108-GPOS-00055, SRG-OS-000375-GPOS-00161"
          }
        ],
        "name": "metadata",
        "namespace": "http://oval.mitre.org/XMLSchema/oval-definitions-5"
      },
      "version": "1"
    },
